Yes — looking at these three classes together, I would centralize the Reactor retry logging rather than adding the same doBeforeRetry() code separately to each class.

You have two different patterns:

TrackingResourceWebClientHelper
        └── retry logic inline

OSPWebClientService
        └── retry logic inline

RetryableMessageProcessor
        └── Spring @Retryable

For the first two, you can create one shared Reactor RetryConfig.

For RetryableMessageProcessor, that's different because it uses Spring Retry @Retryable, so it should use a Spring RetryListener separately.

1. Create a common Reactor RetryConfig

Since these classes are in the same application, create:

config/
    RetryConfig.java

Something like:

package com.cardinalhealth.advship.tracking.data.processor.config;

import lombok.extern.slf4j.Slf4j;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClientResponseException;
import reactor.util.retry.Retry;
import reactor.util.retry.Retry.RetrySignal;

import java.time.Duration;

@Slf4j
@Configuration
public class RetryConfig {

    public Retry createRetry(
            int maxAttempts,
            long initialDelaySeconds,
            String serviceName) {

        return Retry.backoff(
                    maxAttempts,
                    Duration.ofSeconds(initialDelaySeconds)
                )
                .maxBackoff(Duration.ofSeconds(5))
                .jitter(0.5)
                .filter(this::isRetryable)
                .doBeforeRetry(signal ->
                        logRetry(signal, serviceName)
                )
                .onRetryExhaustedThrow((spec, signal) -> {
                    logRetryExhausted(signal, serviceName);

                    Throwable failure = signal.failure();

                    if (failure instanceof RuntimeException) {
                        return failure;
                    }

                    return new RuntimeException(
                            "Retries exhausted for " + serviceName,
                            failure
                    );
                });
    }

    public boolean isRetryable(Throwable throwable) {

        if (throwable instanceof WebClientResponseException ex) {
            int statusCode = ex.getStatusCode().value();

            return statusCode >= 500 && statusCode < 600;
        }

        return true;
    }

    private void logRetry(
            RetrySignal signal,
            String serviceName) {

        Throwable failure = signal.failure();

        log.error(
                "Retrying {}. retryAttempt={}, exceptionType={}, " +
                "message={}, statusCode={}",
                serviceName,
                signal.totalRetries() + 1,
                failure.getClass().getSimpleName(),
                failure.getMessage(),
                getStatusCode(failure),
                failure
        );
    }

    private void logRetryExhausted(
            RetrySignal signal,
            String serviceName) {

        Throwable failure = signal.failure();

        log.error(
                "Retries exhausted for {}. totalRetries={}, " +
                "exceptionType={}, message={}, statusCode={}",
                serviceName,
                signal.totalRetries(),
                failure.getClass().getSimpleName(),
                failure.getMessage(),
                getStatusCode(failure),
                failure
        );
    }

    private Integer getStatusCode(Throwable throwable) {

        if (throwable instanceof WebClientResponseException ex) {
            return ex.getStatusCode().value();
        }

        return null;
    }
}

The important part is that the retry configuration is centralized, while the service name allows you to identify which API is retrying.


