So you'll have two centralized mechanisms

This is the architecture I'd recommend for this application:

RETRY LOGGING
                              │
               ┌──────────────┴──────────────┐
               │                             │
        Reactor Retry                  Spring Retry
               │                             │
               ↓                             ↓
        RetryConfig                  RetryLoggingListener
               │                             │
       ┌───────┴────────┐                    │
       ↓                ↓                    ↓
TrackingResource      OSP             RetryableMessageProcessor
WebClientHelper       WebClient

Why this is better

You don't end up with:

TrackingResourceWebClientHelper
    → retry logging

OSPWebClientService
    → duplicate retry logging

another WebClient
    → duplicate retry logging

Instead:

All Reactor retry clients
        ↓
   RetryConfig
        ↓
 one logging implementation

And your Spring @Retryable code gets its own appropriate listener.

One thing I'd change from my earlier suggestions: don't blindly use log.error() for every retry in every service. Since retryable failures are expected behavior, your team's logging standard may prefer warn for individual retry attempts and error only when retries are exhausted. Check what your existing production logging convention is before finalizing the level.